    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "First, multiply each term in the first binomial by each term in the second binomial. Then, use the distributive property to expand the expression. Simplify the expression by combining like terms.\nTo find the expansion of ${(2x+3)(x+5)}$, we multiply each term in the first binomial by each term in the second binomial:\n\n$$(2x+3)(x+5) = (2x)(x) + (2x)(5) + (3)(x) + (3)(5)$$\n\nNow, use the distributive property to expand each term:\n\n$$(2x)(x) = 2x^2$$\n$$(2x)(5) = 10x$$\n$$(3)(x) = 3x$$\n$$(3)(5) = 15$$\n\nFinally, combine like terms:\n\n$$2x^2 + 10x + 3x + 15$$\n\nSimplify the expression:\n\n$$2x^2 + 13x + 15$$\n\nSo, the expanded form of ${(2x+3)(x+5)}$ is $\\boxed{2x^2+13x+15}$.",
